From 074272bfff9a8d7ff13123efa887778ec528e82a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yang Tsao <yang@fydeos.io>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 14:10:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 50/75] wm: don't clamp an ARC dialog task to the minimum
 resizable size
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The PackageInstaller confirmation kept focus but every tap landed above
the card — "focused, but the input offset is wrong".

The install task asks for 576x368 (its card). adjustForMinimalTaskDimensions
then grows the resolved bounds to the display's default minimum for a
resizable task: mMinSizeOfResizeableTaskDp=320, which at the panel's 288dpi
is 576px. So the task resolves to 576x576 while its requested bounds stay
576x368, and the wrap-content dialog window — centre gravity — is laid out
as frame=[0,104][576,471] inside the taller parent. The compositor places the
card from the requested task footprint, so input sat exactly 104px off:

  task 54 (PackageInstaller, broken)   task 49 (a normal app, fine)
    mBounds      576x368                 mBounds      1600x1000
    winConfig    576x576   <-- grown     winConfig    1600x1000
    frame  [0,104][576,471]              frame  [0,0][1600,1000]

Patch 0043 exempts standalone dialog tasks by forcing them FULLSCREEN, which
returns before the clamp. It explicitly does not cover the translucent
multi-activity case (PackageInstaller's root InstallStaging is not floating),
which stays freeform — so that case reaches the clamp and is the one that
breaks.

The minimum exists so a user cannot resize a task too small to manipulate. An
ARC dialog is a modal sized to its card and is not user-resizable, so the
default does not apply. Skip only the default fill-in; a dialog activity that
declares its own minWidth/minHeight still gets them.

Verified by the user on device: the install confirmation is now tappable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I3a533b1b96aa94dd0a40906a1cf20fefff2bdb04
---
 services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
index bbcb7d81acbc..6a263001c570 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
@@ -2094,10 +2094,21 @@ class Task extends TaskFragment {
             @NonNull Configuration parentConfig) {
         int minWidth = mMinWidth;
         int minHeight = mMinHeight;
+        // fydroid-rt: An ARC dialog task is a centered modal, sized to its card and
+        // not resizable by the user, so the "too small to manipulate" default below
+        // does not apply — and enforcing it breaks input. At 288dpi the default is
+        // 320dp = 576px, so PackageInstaller's requested 576x368 task resolves to
+        // 576x576. Its window is wrap-content with centre gravity, so WM then lays
+        // it out as frame=[0,104][576,471] inside that taller parent, while the
+        // compositor places the card from the requested task footprint — every tap
+        // lands 104px off the card (the task keeps focus, which is why this reads as
+        // "focused but the input offset is wrong"). A dialog whose activity declares
+        // its own minWidth/minHeight still gets them; only the default is skipped.
+        final boolean isContainerDialog = android.os.Build.IS_CONTAINER && isDialogTaskNow();
         // If the task has no requested minimal size, we'd like to enforce a minimal size
         // so that the user can not render the task fragment too small to manipulate. We don't need
         // to do this for the root pinned task as the bounds are controlled by the system.
-        if (!inPinnedWindowingMode()) {
+        if (!inPinnedWindowingMode() && !isContainerDialog) {
             // Use Display specific min sizes when there is one associated with this Task.
             final int defaultMinSizeDp = mDisplayContent == null
                     ? DEFAULT_MIN_TASK_SIZE_DP : mDisplayContent.mMinSizeOfResizeableTaskDp;
